@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+# Samsung supercall dispatcher — 29 October 2026
+
+Base: Samsung source `cc2b27a3f66f11cd9b6bf511a60b8e47c128eae4`.
+Adapted from upstream's table delivery and fallback changes through
+[`2517cae`](https://github.com/inforcqb/susfs4ksu-for-lkm/commit/2517cae8695eac0a294d555718cf280d1d43c8c0),
+including `792ac97`, `f912c9c` and `a8c5d7e`. This is a focused dispatcher port;
+the maps and symbol-listing BTI conversions are separate changes.
+
+## Delivery and ownership
+
+1. Try the reboot entry through the existing Samsung syscall-table helper.
+   Its saved-original publication, verified write/rollback, native ownership
+   checks and complete callback drain remain unchanged. A failed install must
+   restore any possible publication before it can return to the fallback.
+2. Only then consider the existing kprobe delivery. Refuse it when the selected
+   build enables `CONFIG_ARM64_BTI_KERNEL`, the exact reboot body cannot be
+   resolved/read, or that entry begins with explicit `BTI c`/`BTI jc`. Probe the
+   same address that was inspected; there is no second name-based resolution.
+   This is a conservative entry check, not general certification of another KMI.
+3. Both routes call one admission/queue function. Wrong magic, non-root callers,
+   unsupported/inactive commands, initialization, removal, allocation failure
+   and enqueue failure retain the existing forwarding behavior and reference
+   accounting. Payload ABI and the deferred handlers are unchanged.
+4. The table wrapper counts its entire frame, including a potentially sleeping
+   normal reboot call. Queued commands additionally own a module reference
+   through dispatch and work-object cleanup. Initialization cannot publish work.
+5. Teardown restores/drains the table wrapper or unregisters the probe, then
+   waits for the task-work callback's final return tail on either route. There
+   is no timed teardown fallback. The existing rule that live kernel references
+   prevent module text from being freed remains in force.
+
+The root-readable parameter
+`/sys/module/susfs_guard_lkm/parameters/supercall_route` reports `syscall-table`,
+`kprobe` or `none`. It is read-only, including in the runtime parameter API.
+An unconditional warning after successful initialization identifies kprobe
+delivery and its kernel-debugging/instruction-inspection trace. Table delivery
+avoids this particular reboot-entry breakpoint; other module probes remain.
+
+## Validation
+
+- The lifecycle harness now runs 40 cases/configurations in normal and
+  ASan/UBSan builds. Both routes cover queued/sleeping commands, initialization,
+  removal admission, payload/allocation/enqueue failures and the callback tail.
+  Additional cases exercise route selection, full original-register/result
+  forwarding, a sleeping original syscall, the wrapper return tail, failed
+  publication before fallback, BTI refusal, missing/unreadable entries,
+  registration failure and 20 alternating route lifecycles. The admission race
+  runs 200 iterations per route and build mode.
+- Four negative controls remove initialization admission, wrapper counting,
+  task-work tail synchronization or the explicit BTI guard. Each must fail its
+  behavioral test. The real table helper's restoration, late-entry and
+  wake/return-tail cases remain in `tests/fp_hooks`.
+- All 26 host suites are validated in their 50 supported runner configurations.
+  One pre-existing 8192-rule path-listing ASan case exceeded the Python runner's
+  20-second deadline. Its C harness already allowed 120 seconds; the outer
+  deadline now matches that budget only for this sanitizer stress case. The
+  failed attempts and the final targeted rerun are retained in the records.
+- The Android16/6.12 DDK build retains CFI, Tasks RCU, MODVERSIONS and 4 KiB pages.
+  All 14 syscall wrappers match the real kernel-header syscall type and carry
+  call landing instructions. Kernel imports, existing callback CFI types and
+  module version-table shape match the previous Samsung module.
+
+These are host models, compile checks and static artifact checks. They do not
+test Samsung protected-memory writes or live kernel dispatch. No phone load,
+unload, reboot or app test has been performed for this adaptation.
+
+## Reproduce
+
+```sh
+python3 tools/run_host_suite.py --output build/dispatcher-host
+python3 tests/lifecycle/run.py --sanitizers --json build/dispatcher-lifecycle.json
+python3 tools/build_review.py --kdir /opt/ddk/kdir/android16-6.12 \
+  --clang-bin /opt/ddk/clang/clang-r536225/bin \
+  --evidence-dir build/dispatcher-module --omit-module-btf --jobs 2
+python3 tools/check_audit_artifact.py --candidate kernel/susfs_guard_lkm.ko \
+  --baseline /path/to/previous/susfs_guard_lkm.ko \
+  --kdir /opt/ddk/kdir/android16-6.12 \
+  --clang-bin /opt/ddk/clang/clang-r536225/bin --output build/dispatcher-artifact
+```
+
+Provision the pinned DDK using the app repository's `tools/provision_ddk.py`.
+The artifact checker needs `pyelftools`. Preserve an unstripped artifact for
+investigation; use the pinned `llvm-strip -d` for distribution.
+
+For phone validation, install the test APK, fully reboot, restore root and
+activate SusFS. Read `supercall_route`, verify the app's status and the manager's
+normal version/feature/rule commands, then check the configured modules after
+the normal soft restart. A soft restart alone retains the previously loaded
+SusFS module and cannot replace it with this build.
